# config/kernel/Kconfig.livepatch — Live update (kernel live patches)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signed kernel livepatch (Level 2 live update; kernel/src/boot/livepatch.rs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config LIVEPATCH
    bool "Signed kernel livepatch of non-sleeping functions"
    depends on RCU_QSBR && LIVEPATCH_POOL_KIB != 0 && LIVEPATCH_ENTRY_NOPS != 0
    default n
    help
      Admits a signed patch blob (a replacement body per function) named by
      a `[livepatch.NAME]` row of the signed topology, and redirects the
      entry of each function it replaces: one `B` on aarch64, one `JAL` on
      riscv64 (within 1 MiB of the pool), the `int3` sequence on x86_64.
      The bodies go into `.text.patch_pool` through the one text writer
      (crates/core/mm/src/text_poke.rs), so the kernel text stays RX and
      `verify_no_exec_outside_image` is unchanged. A blob is applied only
      when: its RSIG signature (the update key) verifies, a row names its
      SHA-256, it is built for this ISA and this kernel's build-id
      (`[PATCH] build=` on the boot line, tools/check_livepatch_layout.py),
      its `sec_epoch` is not below the OTA floor, and every function is in
      the image's allowlist (non-sleeping, `#[inline(never)]` entry points
      with an entry slot in `.azos_patch`). One bounded QSBR grace follows
      the redirect (LIVEPATCH_GRACE_MS); no CPU is ever stopped. Nothing
      persists across a reboot: the signed rows are re-applied at boot.

      n (default, and what a BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE build gets unless it
      asks otherwise): the code is not compiled in, the build-id is not
      computed, and a topology that declares `livepatch` rows has each one
      refused at boot with a decision record. y needs the pool
      (LIVEPATCH_POOL_KIB) and the entry slots (LIVEPATCH_ENTRY_NOPS: 1
      on aarch64, 3 on riscv64 where functions are 2-aligned, and on x86_64
      one 5-byte nop) in config/kernel/Kconfig.livepatch.

      Cost with y: one SHA-256 pass over the whole kernel text (about 2 MiB)
      at boot to get the build-id, its time to be measured in the testing
      week; and the patch table (LIVEPATCH_MAX_PATCHES), 8 B plus
      8 + 8 * LIVEPATCH_MAX_FUNCS B per entry. Without it: 8 B.

config LIVEPATCH_GRACE_MS
    int "Livepatch: the bounded grace period after a redirect (ms)"
    range 1 10000
    default 200
    help
      How long an apply waits for every other CPU to pass a
      quiescent state (qsbr::synchronize_rcu_for) before it records the old
      body as possibly still running. The redirect is already in force and
      a miss does not undo it. Set it above the longest time a CPU runs kernel
      code without a context switch, an interrupt return or idle (an RT CPU
      in user mode is quiescent at once). Unused without LIVEPATCH.

config LIVEPATCH_MAX_PATCHES
    int "Livepatch: patches applied at once"
    range 1 32
    default 4
    help
      Size of the patch table, which holds the entries each patch has
      claimed (before its first write, and while it is in force). A patch
      that does not fit is refused. Never useful above the topology's
      `[livepatch.NAME]` rows (TOPOLOGY_MAX_LIVEPATCHES).

      Cost: 8 + 8 * LIVEPATCH_MAX_FUNCS B of RAM per entry (72 B with the
      default of 8 functions), either ISA; none without LIVEPATCH.

config LIVEPATCH_MAX_FUNCS
    int "Livepatch: functions one patch may replace"
    range 1 64
    default 8
    help
      Bound on the function table of one blob; a blob that names more is
      refused as malformed. Unused without LIVEPATCH.

config LIVEPATCH_MAX_BLOB_KIB
    int "Livepatch: largest patch blob (KiB)"
    range 1 1024
    default 64
    help
      The boot re-apply reads a blob from `/fat/PATCH/` into a buffer of
      this size, taken from the kernel heap for the read and given back
      after it; a larger file is refused. The sum of the bodies of the
      applied patches is bounded by LIVEPATCH_POOL_KIB instead. Unused
      without LIVEPATCH.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live-update build hooks (owner plan, Level 2 livepatch)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config LIVEPATCH_POOL_KIB
    int "Live-update patch pool (KiB of RX space in the kernel image)"
    range 0 4096
    default 0
    help
      Size of `.text.patch_pool`, the executable output section every kernel
      linker script places inside the image's text range (before `_text_end`,
      so the W^X and no-exec-outside-the-image checks cover it) between
      `__patch_pool_start` and `__patch_pool_end`: the room a replacement
      function body is loaded into later. 0 (default) = absent: the section is
      empty and costs no byte. Zero-filled, so a stray jump into it traps.
      Reaches the linker as `--defsym=__patch_pool_bytes` through
      tools/kconfig_to_cargo.py --rustflags; the `[PATCH]` boot line reports
      the size the linker actually laid out. Cost: this many KiB of image.

config LIVEPATCH_ENTRY_NOPS
    int "Live-update entry NOPs per function (patchable-function-entry)"
    range 0 16
    default 0
    help
      N > 0 builds the kernel with `-Z patchable-function-entry=N,0`: N NOP
      instructions at the entry of every function, which a live patch
      overwrites with a jump to the replacement in `.text.patch_pool`. 0
      (default) = no NOPs and no code change. The compiler lists each
      function's entry in `__patchable_function_entries`, which the linker
      scripts gather into `.azos_patch` (one 8-byte entry address per site;
      the `[PATCH]` boot line counts them). Cost: N instructions per
      function call (N * 2 or 4 bytes of text per function). The
      nightly-only flag is why this is a build setting.

config LIVEPATCH_FP
    bool "Live-update frame pointers (-C force-frame-pointers=yes)"
    default n
    help
      Keeps a frame pointer in every function, so a patcher can walk a stack
      and see whether a function it is replacing is still on it. Off
      (default): the code is unchanged. Cost: one register and a prologue
      and epilogue store/load pair per non-leaf function.
